# Sprint 26: the reverse proxy and TLS terminator app/main.py's enforcement_middleware has been
# waiting for since Sprint 21 (see its comment: "the real TLS certificate and reverse proxy are
# still Paso 25's job"). Caddy is the terminator; the backend still enforces `https_required` on
# anything that reaches it without a `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` header, which `reverse_proxy`
# below sets automatically.
#
# WEB_DOMAIN/API_DOMAIN drive Caddy's own automatic-HTTPS decision, not just routing: for a real
# public domain (the production case — set these to your two real subdomains, matching
# CORS_ORIGINS/ALLOWED_HOSTS/NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL in .env.production) Caddy provisions and
# renews a real Let's Encrypt certificate via ACME with no extra config. For `web.localhost`/
# `api.localhost` (this repo's default, used for local verification without owning a domain —
# see docs/sprint-26-evidence.md) Caddy recognizes the `.localhost` TLD as never publicly
# resolvable (RFC 6761) and transparently issues from its own internal CA instead of trying ACME
# against a name that could never pass an HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01 challenge. Same Caddyfile, same
# `reverse_proxy` behavior either way — only the certificate issuer changes, automatically.
{$WEB_DOMAIN} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}

{$API_DOMAIN} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Verified for real (docs/sprint-26-evidence.md) that a bare `reverse_proxy backend:8000`
	# below, with no path restriction, happily proxied GET /metrics too — a plain `reverse_proxy`
	# forwards every path on the site by default, it does not know or care that /metrics is meant
	# to stay private-network-only. Without this block, this site would be the second, unintended
	# route to it (compose.prod.yaml not publishing a host port for backend is the *other* one,
	# and still holds — this closes the one Caddy itself was opening).
	@metrics path /metrics
	respond @metrics 404

	reverse_proxy backend:8000
}
